{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack Hotkeys supports multi-key sequences, shortcuts where you press keys one after another rather than together. Think Vim-style navigation, cheat codes, or multi-step commands."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Sequence steps use the same string syntax as single hotkeys. For example, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"['[KeyG]', '[KeyG]']"},{"type":"text","value":" follows a physical position, while "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"['G', 'G']"},{"type":"text","value":" follows the logical letter. A sequence can mix forms, such as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"['Mod+[KeyK]', 'C']"},{"type":"text","value":". Display steps with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sequence.map((step) => formatForDisplay(step)).join(' → ')"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"basic-usage","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Basic usage"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useHotkeySequence"},{"type":"text","value":" hook to register a key sequence:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { useHotkeySequence } from '@tanstack/react-hotkeys'\n\nfunction App() {\n  // Vim-style: press g then g to scroll to top\n  useHotkeySequence(['G', 'G'], () => {\n    window.scrollTo({ top: 0, behavior: 'smooth' })\n  })\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The first argument is an array of "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Hotkey"},{"type":"text","value":" strings representing each step in the sequence. The user must press them in order within the timeout window."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"many-sequences-at-once","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Many sequences at once"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When you need several sequences, or a dynamic list whose length isn't fixed at compile time, use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useHotkeySequences"},{"type":"text","value":" instead of calling "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useHotkeySequence"},{"type":"text","value":" many times. One hook call keeps you within the rules of hooks while still registering every sequence."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { useHotkeySequences } from '@tanstack/react-hotkeys'\n\nuseHotkeySequences([\n  { sequence: ['G', 'G'], callback: () => scrollToTop() },\n  { sequence: ['D', 'D'], callback: () => deleteLine(), options: { timeout: 500 } },\n])","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Options merge in the same order as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useHotkeys"},{"type":"text","value":": "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"HotkeysProvider"},{"type":"text","value":" defaults, then the second-argument "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"commonOptions"},{"type":"text","value":", then each definition's "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"options"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"sequence-options","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Sequence options"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The third argument is an options object:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"useHotkeySequence(['G', 'G'], callback, {\n  timeout: 1000,  // Time allowed between keys (ms)\n  enabled: true,  // Whether the sequence is active\n})","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"timeout","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"timeout"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The maximum time (in milliseconds) allowed between consecutive key presses. If the user takes longer than this between any two keys, the sequence resets. Defaults to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"1000"},{"type":"text","value":" (1 second)."}]},{"type":"code","value":"// Fast sequence - user must type quickly\nuseHotkeySequence(['D', 'D'], () => deleteLine(), { timeout: 500 })\n\n// Slow sequence - user has more time between keys\nuseHotkeySequence(['Shift+Z', 'Shift+Z'], () => forceQuit(), { timeout: 2000 })","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"enabled","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"enabled"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Controls whether the sequence is active. Defaults to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"true"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Disabled sequences stay registered and visible in devtools; only execution is suppressed."}]},{"type":"code","value":"const [isVimMode, setIsVimMode] = useState(true)\n\nuseHotkeySequence(['G', 'G'], () => scrollToTop(), { enabled: isVimMode })","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"global-defaults-via-provider","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Global defaults via provider"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You can set default options for all "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useHotkeySequence"},{"type":"text","value":" calls by wrapping your component tree with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"HotkeysProvider"},{"type":"text","value":". Per-hook options override the provider defaults."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { HotkeysProvider } from '@tanstack/react-hotkeys'\n\n<HotkeysProvider\n  defaultOptions={{\n    hotkeySequence: { timeout: 1500 },\n  }}\n>\n  <App />\n</HotkeysProvider>","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"meta","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"meta"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Sequences support the same "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"meta"},{"type":"text","value":" option as hotkeys. Attach a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"name"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"description"},{"type":"text","value":" for use in shortcut palettes and devtools."}]},{"type":"code","value":"useHotkeySequence(['G', 'G'], () => scrollToTop(), {\n  meta: { name: 'Go to Top', description: 'Scroll to the top of the page' },\n})","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"See the "},{"type":"link","href":"./hotkeys.md#metadata-name--description","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Hotkeys Guide"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for details on declaration merging and introspecting registrations."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"sequences-with-modifiers","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Sequences with modifiers"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Each step in a sequence can include modifiers:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"// Ctrl+K followed by Ctrl+C (VS Code-style comment)\nuseHotkeySequence(['Mod+K', 'Mod+C'], () => {\n  commentSelection()\n})\n\n// g then Shift+G (go to bottom, Vim-style)\nuseHotkeySequence(['G', 'Shift+G'], () => {\n  scrollToBottom()\n})","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"chained-modifier-chords","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Chained modifier chords"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This example follows physical R and T positions. Brackets retain those positions even when the keys produce different letters. Other sequences can continue using logical characters."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You can repeat the same modifier across consecutive steps, for example "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Shift+R"},{"type":"text","value":" then "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Shift+T"},{"type":"text","value":":"}]},{"type":"code","value":"useHotkeySequence(['Shift+[KeyR]', 'Shift+[KeyT]'], () => {\n  doNextAction()\n})","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"modifier-only-keys-between-steps","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Modifier-only keys between steps"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"While a sequence is in progress, modifier-only keydown events (Shift, Control, Alt, or Meta pressed alone, with no other key) are ignored. They neither advance the sequence nor reset progress. A user can tap Shift, or hold it, between chords like "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Shift+R"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Shift+T"},{"type":"text","value":" without breaking the sequence, matching Vim-style flows where a modifier goes down before the next chord."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"common-sequence-patterns","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Common sequence patterns"}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"vim-style-navigation","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Vim-style navigation"}]},{"type":"code","value":"function VimNavigation() {\n  useHotkeySequence(['G', 'G'], () => scrollToTop())\n  useHotkeySequence(['G', 'Shift+G'], () => scrollToBottom())\n  useHotkeySequence(['D', 'D'], () => deleteLine())\n  useHotkeySequence(['D', 'W'], () => deleteWord())\n  useHotkeySequence(['C', 'I', 'W'], () => changeInnerWord())\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"konami-code","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Konami Code"}]},{"type":"code","value":"useHotkeySequence(\n  [\n    'ArrowUp', 'ArrowUp',\n    'ArrowDown', 'ArrowDown',\n    'ArrowLeft', 'ArrowRight',\n    'ArrowLeft', 'ArrowRight',\n    'B', 'A',\n  ],\n  () => enableEasterEgg(),\n  { timeout: 2000 },\n)","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"multi-step-commands","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Multi-step commands"}]},{"type":"code","value":"// Press \"h\", \"e\", \"l\", \"p\" to open help\nuseHotkeySequence(['H', 'E', 'L', 'P'], () => openHelp())","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"how-sequences-work","children":[{"type":"text","value":"How sequences work"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Both "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"SequenceManager"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createSequenceMatcher"},{"type":"text","value":" ignore modifier-only events, IME composition, and automatic keydown repeats. These events neither advance the sequence nor refresh its timeout: holding G does not complete a two-press G sequence. The manager prefers exact matches over weaker logical-key fallbacks while preserving equally strong matches."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"SequenceManager"},{"type":"text","value":" (singleton) handles all sequence registrations. When a key is pressed:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"It checks if the key matches the next expected step in any registered sequence"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If it matches, the sequence advances to the next step"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If the timeout expires between steps, the sequence resets"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When all steps are completed, the callback fires"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Modifier-only keydowns are ignored (they neither advance nor reset the sequence)"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"overlapping-sequences","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Overlapping sequences"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Multiple sequences can share the same prefix. The manager tracks progress for each sequence independently:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"// Both share the 'D' prefix\nuseHotkeySequence(['D', 'D'], () => deleteLine())   // dd\nuseHotkeySequence(['D', 'W'], () => deleteWord())    // dw\nuseHotkeySequence(['D', 'I', 'W'], () => deleteInnerWord()) // diw","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"After pressing "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"D"},{"type":"text","value":", the manager waits for the next key to determine which sequence to complete."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"conflicting-registrations","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Conflicting registrations"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For the same target, duplicate detection compares resolved steps: modifier aliases, modifier order, and logical key casing do not create separate bindings. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"conflictBehavior"},{"type":"text","value":" applies to equivalent sequences without rewriting their stored strings. Physical and logical identities remain distinct, and a shared prefix alone is not a duplicate registration. Recorder conflict detection also checks prefixes and observed physical/logical overlap."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"the-sequence-manager","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The sequence manager"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Under the hood, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useHotkeySequence"},{"type":"text","value":" uses the singleton "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"SequenceManager"},{"type":"text","value":". For standalone sequence matching without the singleton, use the core "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createSequenceMatcher"},{"type":"text","value":" function:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { createSequenceMatcher } from '@tanstack/react-hotkeys'\n\nconst matcher = createSequenceMatcher(['G', 'G'], {\n  timeout: 1000,\n})\n\ndocument.addEventListener('keydown', (e) => {\n  if (matcher.match(e)) {\n    console.log('Sequence completed!')\n  }\n  console.log('Progress:', matcher.getProgress()) // e.g., 1/2\n})","lang":"tsx"}],"headings":[{"id":"basic-usage","text":"Basic usage","level":2},{"id":"many-sequences-at-once","text":"Many sequences at once","level":2},{"id":"sequence-options","text":"Sequence options","level":2},{"id":"timeout","text":"timeout","level":3},{"id":"enabled","text":"enabled","level":3},{"id":"global-defaults-via-provider","text":"Global defaults via provider","level":3},{"id":"meta","text":"meta","level":3},{"id":"sequences-with-modifiers","text":"Sequences with modifiers","level":2},{"id":"chained-modifier-chords","text":"Chained modifier chords","level":2},{"id":"modifier-only-keys-between-steps","text":"Modifier-only keys between steps","level":3},{"id":"common-sequence-patterns","text":"Common sequence patterns","level":2},{"id":"vim-style-navigation","text":"Vim-style navigation","level":3},{"id":"konami-code","text":"Konami Code","level":3},{"id":"multi-step-commands","text":"Multi-step commands","level":3},{"id":"how-sequences-work","text":"How sequences work","level":2},{"id":"overlapping-sequences","text":"Overlapping sequences","level":3},{"id":"conflicting-registrations","text":"Conflicting registrations","level":3},{"id":"the-sequence-manager","text":"The sequence manager","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\nTanStack Hotkeys supports multi-key sequences, shortcuts where you press keys one after another rather than together. Think Vim-style navigation, cheat codes, or multi-step commands.\n\nSequence steps use the same string syntax as single hotkeys. For example, `['[KeyG]', '[KeyG]']` follows a physical position, while `['G', 'G']` follows the logical letter. A sequence can mix forms, such as `['Mod+[KeyK]', 'C']`. Display steps with `sequence.map((step) => formatForDisplay(step)).join(' → ')`.\n\n## Basic usage\n\nUse the `useHotkeySequence` hook to register a key sequence:\n\n```tsx\nimport { useHotkeySequence } from '@tanstack/react-hotkeys'\n\nfunction App() {\n  // Vim-style: press g then g to scroll to top\n  useHotkeySequence(['G', 'G'], () => {\n    window.scrollTo({ top: 0, behavior: 'smooth' })\n  })\n}\n```\n\nThe first argument is an array of `Hotkey` strings representing each step in the sequence. The user must press them in order within the timeout window.\n\n## Many sequences at once\n\nWhen you need several sequences, or a dynamic list whose length isn't fixed at compile time, use `useHotkeySequences` instead of calling `useHotkeySequence` many times. One hook call keeps you within the rules of hooks while still registering every sequence.\n\n```tsx\nimport { useHotkeySequences } from '@tanstack/react-hotkeys'\n\nuseHotkeySequences([\n  { sequence: ['G', 'G'], callback: () => scrollToTop() },\n  { sequence: ['D', 'D'], callback: () => deleteLine(), options: { timeout: 500 } },\n])\n```\n\nOptions merge in the same order as `useHotkeys`: `HotkeysProvider` defaults, then the second-argument `commonOptions`, then each definition's `options`.\n\n## Sequence options\n\nThe third argument is an options object:\n\n```tsx\nuseHotkeySequence(['G', 'G'], callback, {\n  timeout: 1000,  // Time allowed between keys (ms)\n  enabled: true,  // Whether the sequence is active\n})\n```\n\n### `timeout`\n\nThe maximum time (in milliseconds) allowed between consecutive key presses. If the user takes longer than this between any two keys, the sequence resets. Defaults to `1000` (1 second).\n\n```tsx\n// Fast sequence - user must type quickly\nuseHotkeySequence(['D', 'D'], () => deleteLine(), { timeout: 500 })\n\n// Slow sequence - user has more time between keys\nuseHotkeySequence(['Shift+Z', 'Shift+Z'], () => forceQuit(), { timeout: 2000 })\n```\n\n### `enabled`\n\nControls whether the sequence is active. Defaults to `true`.\n\nDisabled sequences stay registered and visible in devtools; only execution is suppressed.\n\n```tsx\nconst [isVimMode, setIsVimMode] = useState(true)\n\nuseHotkeySequence(['G', 'G'], () => scrollToTop(), { enabled: isVimMode })\n```\n\n### Global defaults via provider\n\nYou can set default options for all `useHotkeySequence` calls by wrapping your component tree with `HotkeysProvider`. Per-hook options override the provider defaults.\n\n```tsx\nimport { HotkeysProvider } from '@tanstack/react-hotkeys'\n\n<HotkeysProvider\n  defaultOptions={{\n    hotkeySequence: { timeout: 1500 },\n  }}\n>\n  <App />\n</HotkeysProvider>\n```\n\n### `meta`\n\nSequences support the same `meta` option as hotkeys. Attach a `name` and `description` for use in shortcut palettes and devtools.\n\n```tsx\nuseHotkeySequence(['G', 'G'], () => scrollToTop(), {\n  meta: { name: 'Go to Top', description: 'Scroll to the top of the page' },\n})\n```\n\nSee the [Hotkeys Guide](./hotkeys.md#metadata-name--description) for details on declaration merging and introspecting registrations.\n\n## Sequences with modifiers\n\nEach step in a sequence can include modifiers:\n\n```tsx\n// Ctrl+K followed by Ctrl+C (VS Code-style comment)\nuseHotkeySequence(['Mod+K', 'Mod+C'], () => {\n  commentSelection()\n})\n\n// g then Shift+G (go to bottom, Vim-style)\nuseHotkeySequence(['G', 'Shift+G'], () => {\n  scrollToBottom()\n})\n```\n\n## Chained modifier chords\n\nThis example follows physical R and T positions. Brackets retain those positions even when the keys produce different letters. Other sequences can continue using logical characters.\n\nYou can repeat the same modifier across consecutive steps, for example `Shift+R` then `Shift+T`:\n\n```tsx\nuseHotkeySequence(['Shift+[KeyR]', 'Shift+[KeyT]'], () => {\n  doNextAction()\n})\n```\n\n### Modifier-only keys between steps\n\nWhile a sequence is in progress, modifier-only keydown events (Shift, Control, Alt, or Meta pressed alone, with no other key) are ignored. They neither advance the sequence nor reset progress. A user can tap Shift, or hold it, between chords like `Shift+R` and `Shift+T` without breaking the sequence, matching Vim-style flows where a modifier goes down before the next chord.\n\n## Common sequence patterns\n\n### Vim-style navigation\n\n```tsx\nfunction VimNavigation() {\n  useHotkeySequence(['G', 'G'], () => scrollToTop())\n  useHotkeySequence(['G', 'Shift+G'], () => scrollToBottom())\n  useHotkeySequence(['D', 'D'], () => deleteLine())\n  useHotkeySequence(['D', 'W'], () => deleteWord())\n  useHotkeySequence(['C', 'I', 'W'], () => changeInnerWord())\n}\n```\n\n### Konami Code\n\n```tsx\nuseHotkeySequence(\n  [\n    'ArrowUp', 'ArrowUp',\n    'ArrowDown', 'ArrowDown',\n    'ArrowLeft', 'ArrowRight',\n    'ArrowLeft', 'ArrowRight',\n    'B', 'A',\n  ],\n  () => enableEasterEgg(),\n  { timeout: 2000 },\n)\n```\n\n### Multi-step commands\n\n```tsx\n// Press \"h\", \"e\", \"l\", \"p\" to open help\nuseHotkeySequence(['H', 'E', 'L', 'P'], () => openHelp())\n```\n\n## How sequences work\n\nBoth `SequenceManager` and `createSequenceMatcher` ignore modifier-only events, IME composition, and automatic keydown repeats. These events neither advance the sequence nor refresh its timeout: holding G does not complete a two-press G sequence. The manager prefers exact matches over weaker logical-key fallbacks while preserving equally strong matches.\n\nThe `SequenceManager` (singleton) handles all sequence registrations. When a key is pressed:\n\n1. It checks if the key matches the next expected step in any registered sequence\n2. If it matches, the sequence advances to the next step\n3. If the timeout expires between steps, the sequence resets\n4. When all steps are completed, the callback fires\n5. Modifier-only keydowns are ignored (they neither advance nor reset the sequence)\n\n### Overlapping sequences\n\nMultiple sequences can share the same prefix. The manager tracks progress for each sequence independently:\n\n```tsx\n// Both share the 'D' prefix\nuseHotkeySequence(['D', 'D'], () => deleteLine())   // dd\nuseHotkeySequence(['D', 'W'], () => deleteWord())    // dw\nuseHotkeySequence(['D', 'I', 'W'], () => deleteInnerWord()) // diw\n```\n\nAfter pressing `D`, the manager waits for the next key to determine which sequence to complete.\n\n### Conflicting registrations\n\nFor the same target, duplicate detection compares resolved steps: modifier aliases, modifier order, and logical key casing do not create separate bindings. `conflictBehavior` applies to equivalent sequences without rewriting their stored strings. Physical and logical identities remain distinct, and a shared prefix alone is not a duplicate registration. Recorder conflict detection also checks prefixes and observed physical/logical overlap.\n\n## The sequence manager\n\nUnder the hood, `useHotkeySequence` uses the singleton `SequenceManager`. For standalone sequence matching without the singleton, use the core `createSequenceMatcher` function:\n\n```tsx\nimport { createSequenceMatcher } from '@tanstack/react-hotkeys'\n\nconst matcher = createSequenceMatcher(['G', 'G'], {\n  timeout: 1000,\n})\n\ndocument.addEventListener('keydown', (e) => {\n  if (matcher.match(e)) {\n    console.log('Sequence completed!')\n  }\n  console.log('Progress:', matcher.getProgress()) // e.g., 1/2\n})\n```\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\ntitle: Sequences Guide\nid: sequences\n---\n\nTanStack Hotkeys supports multi-key sequences, shortcuts where you press keys one after another rather than together. Think Vim-style navigation, cheat codes, or multi-step commands.\n\nSequence steps use the same string syntax as single hotkeys. For example, `['[KeyG]', '[KeyG]']` follows a physical position, while `['G', 'G']` follows the logical letter. A sequence can mix forms, such as `['Mod+[KeyK]', 'C']`. Display steps with `sequence.map((step) => formatForDisplay(step)).join(' → ')`.\n\n## Basic usage\n\nUse the `useHotkeySequence` hook to register a key sequence:\n\n```tsx\nimport { useHotkeySequence } from '@tanstack/react-hotkeys'\n\nfunction App() {\n  // Vim-style: press g then g to scroll to top\n  useHotkeySequence(['G', 'G'], () => {\n    window.scrollTo({ top: 0, behavior: 'smooth' })\n  })\n}\n```\n\nThe first argument is an array of `Hotkey` strings representing each step in the sequence. The user must press them in order within the timeout window.\n\n## Many sequences at once\n\nWhen you need several sequences, or a dynamic list whose length isn't fixed at compile time, use `useHotkeySequences` instead of calling `useHotkeySequence` many times. One hook call keeps you within the rules of hooks while still registering every sequence.\n\n```tsx\nimport { useHotkeySequences } from '@tanstack/react-hotkeys'\n\nuseHotkeySequences([\n  { sequence: ['G', 'G'], callback: () => scrollToTop() },\n  { sequence: ['D', 'D'], callback: () => deleteLine(), options: { timeout: 500 } },\n])\n```\n\nOptions merge in the same order as `useHotkeys`: `HotkeysProvider` defaults, then the second-argument `commonOptions`, then each definition's `options`.\n\n## Sequence options\n\nThe third argument is an options object:\n\n```tsx\nuseHotkeySequence(['G', 'G'], callback, {\n  timeout: 1000,  // Time allowed between keys (ms)\n  enabled: true,  // Whether the sequence is active\n})\n```\n\n### `timeout`\n\nThe maximum time (in milliseconds) allowed between consecutive key presses. If the user takes longer than this between any two keys, the sequence resets. Defaults to `1000` (1 second).\n\n```tsx\n// Fast sequence - user must type quickly\nuseHotkeySequence(['D', 'D'], () => deleteLine(), { timeout: 500 })\n\n// Slow sequence - user has more time between keys\nuseHotkeySequence(['Shift+Z', 'Shift+Z'], () => forceQuit(), { timeout: 2000 })\n```\n\n### `enabled`\n\nControls whether the sequence is active. Defaults to `true`.\n\nDisabled sequences stay registered and visible in devtools; only execution is suppressed.\n\n```tsx\nconst [isVimMode, setIsVimMode] = useState(true)\n\nuseHotkeySequence(['G', 'G'], () => scrollToTop(), { enabled: isVimMode })\n```\n\n### Global defaults via provider\n\nYou can set default options for all `useHotkeySequence` calls by wrapping your component tree with `HotkeysProvider`. Per-hook options override the provider defaults.\n\n```tsx\nimport { HotkeysProvider } from '@tanstack/react-hotkeys'\n\n<HotkeysProvider\n  defaultOptions={{\n    hotkeySequence: { timeout: 1500 },\n  }}\n>\n  <App />\n</HotkeysProvider>\n```\n\n### `meta`\n\nSequences support the same `meta` option as hotkeys. Attach a `name` and `description` for use in shortcut palettes and devtools.\n\n```tsx\nuseHotkeySequence(['G', 'G'], () => scrollToTop(), {\n  meta: { name: 'Go to Top', description: 'Scroll to the top of the page' },\n})\n```\n\nSee the [Hotkeys Guide](./hotkeys.md#metadata-name--description) for details on declaration merging and introspecting registrations.\n\n## Sequences with modifiers\n\nEach step in a sequence can include modifiers:\n\n```tsx\n// Ctrl+K followed by Ctrl+C (VS Code-style comment)\nuseHotkeySequence(['Mod+K', 'Mod+C'], () => {\n  commentSelection()\n})\n\n// g then Shift+G (go to bottom, Vim-style)\nuseHotkeySequence(['G', 'Shift+G'], () => {\n  scrollToBottom()\n})\n```\n\n## Chained modifier chords\n\nThis example follows physical R and T positions. Brackets retain those positions even when the keys produce different letters. Other sequences can continue using logical characters.\n\nYou can repeat the same modifier across consecutive steps, for example `Shift+R` then `Shift+T`:\n\n```tsx\nuseHotkeySequence(['Shift+[KeyR]', 'Shift+[KeyT]'], () => {\n  doNextAction()\n})\n```\n\n### Modifier-only keys between steps\n\nWhile a sequence is in progress, modifier-only keydown events (Shift, Control, Alt, or Meta pressed alone, with no other key) are ignored. They neither advance the sequence nor reset progress. A user can tap Shift, or hold it, between chords like `Shift+R` and `Shift+T` without breaking the sequence, matching Vim-style flows where a modifier goes down before the next chord.\n\n## Common sequence patterns\n\n### Vim-style navigation\n\n```tsx\nfunction VimNavigation() {\n  useHotkeySequence(['G', 'G'], () => scrollToTop())\n  useHotkeySequence(['G', 'Shift+G'], () => scrollToBottom())\n  useHotkeySequence(['D', 'D'], () => deleteLine())\n  useHotkeySequence(['D', 'W'], () => deleteWord())\n  useHotkeySequence(['C', 'I', 'W'], () => changeInnerWord())\n}\n```\n\n### Konami Code\n\n```tsx\nuseHotkeySequence(\n  [\n    'ArrowUp', 'ArrowUp',\n    'ArrowDown', 'ArrowDown',\n    'ArrowLeft', 'ArrowRight',\n    'ArrowLeft', 'ArrowRight',\n    'B', 'A',\n  ],\n  () => enableEasterEgg(),\n  { timeout: 2000 },\n)\n```\n\n### Multi-step commands\n\n```tsx\n// Press \"h\", \"e\", \"l\", \"p\" to open help\nuseHotkeySequence(['H', 'E', 'L', 'P'], () => openHelp())\n```\n\n## How sequences work\n\nBoth `SequenceManager` and `createSequenceMatcher` ignore modifier-only events, IME composition, and automatic keydown repeats. These events neither advance the sequence nor refresh its timeout: holding G does not complete a two-press G sequence. The manager prefers exact matches over weaker logical-key fallbacks while preserving equally strong matches.\n\nThe `SequenceManager` (singleton) handles all sequence registrations. When a key is pressed:\n\n1. It checks if the key matches the next expected step in any registered sequence\n2. If it matches, the sequence advances to the next step\n3. If the timeout expires between steps, the sequence resets\n4. When all steps are completed, the callback fires\n5. Modifier-only keydowns are ignored (they neither advance nor reset the sequence)\n\n### Overlapping sequences\n\nMultiple sequences can share the same prefix. The manager tracks progress for each sequence independently:\n\n```tsx\n// Both share the 'D' prefix\nuseHotkeySequence(['D', 'D'], () => deleteLine())   // dd\nuseHotkeySequence(['D', 'W'], () => deleteWord())    // dw\nuseHotkeySequence(['D', 'I', 'W'], () => deleteInnerWord()) // diw\n```\n\nAfter pressing `D`, the manager waits for the next key to determine which sequence to complete.\n\n### Conflicting registrations\n\nFor the same target, duplicate detection compares resolved steps: modifier aliases, modifier order, and logical key casing do not create separate bindings. `conflictBehavior` applies to equivalent sequences without rewriting their stored strings. Physical and logical identities remain distinct, and a shared prefix alone is not a duplicate registration. Recorder conflict detection also checks prefixes and observed physical/logical overlap.\n\n## The sequence manager\n\nUnder the hood, `useHotkeySequence` uses the singleton `SequenceManager`. For standalone sequence matching without the singleton, use the core `createSequenceMatcher` function:\n\n```tsx\nimport { createSequenceMatcher } from '@tanstack/react-hotkeys'\n\nconst matcher = createSequenceMatcher(['G', 'G'], {\n  timeout: 1000,\n})\n\ndocument.addEventListener('keydown', (e) => {\n  if (matcher.match(e)) {\n    console.log('Sequence completed!')\n  }\n  console.log('Progress:', matcher.getProgress()) // e.g., 1/2\n})\n```\n"}
